Kenton has been preaching this for years. Every developer lives with low-grade paranoia about environment leaking. Bindings remove the fear by eliminating the thing that can leak, meaning there is no secret to lose.

In Workers, the environment (`env` object) contains live objects, known as bindings, which represent storage namespaces without requiring connection strings or secret tokens. This design prevents the leakage of sensitive information. Despite initial skepticism about this approach, the rise of AI agents has heightened concerns about key leakage, making the Workers design increasingly relevant. Kenton points out that they solved this issue eight years ago.

@KentonVarda: "It's kind of crazy how much of the way we've been designing Workers over the past 9 years unexpectedly turns out to be so relevant to AI and agents. Durable Objects and lightweight isolate sandboxes are obvious big things. But there are subtler things."

